(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-pericardial-disease-quiz"]={"title":"Pericardial Disease Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which laboratory finding is often present in constrictive pericarditis?","choices":["Low albumin with heavy proteinuria","Raised sedimentation rate and C-reactive protein","A natriuretic peptide above a diagnostic cutoff","Positive blood cultures in most patients"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The inflammatory markers erythrocyte sedimentation rate and C-reactive protein are often elevated, while no natriuretic peptide threshold confirms or excludes the diagnosis. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Which situation is an indication to consider pericardiocentesis or pericardiectomy in pericarditis?","choices":["Pain relieved after 2 days of therapy","Suspected bacterial cause, large effusion","A first viral episode with a friction rub","Diffuse ST elevation with no effusion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Drainage or pericardiectomy is considered for a moderate to large effusion with tamponade or refractory symptoms, suspected neoplastic or bacterial disease with a moderate to large effusion, frequent symptomatic recurrences with effusion, or constriction. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"In the C-reactive protein approach to therapy, how often is the C-reactive protein reassessed after presentation?","choices":["Weekly","Daily","Monthly","Every 3 months"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. C-reactive protein is assessed at presentation and then weekly, and tapering starts once the patient is symptom-free and the level has normalized. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which feature predicts a poorer prognosis in acute pericarditis?","choices":["Prompt response within a week","Subacute onset over days to weeks","Pain eased by sitting forward","A preceding viral illness"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A subacute onset is among the poor-prognosis features, along with fever above 38, a large effusion, no response after 1 week, myopericarditis, trauma and oral anticoagulation. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Which imaging helps evaluate pericardial thickness when constriction is suspected but unconfirmed?","choices":["Ventilation-perfusion scan or plain radiograph","Exercise treadmill test or stress echocardiogram","Abdominal ultrasound and liver elastography","Cardiac magnetic resonance or computed tomography"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Cardiac magnetic resonance or computed tomography helps evaluate pericardial thickness, especially when the diagnosis is suspected but unconfirmed, and can show active inflammation. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Hoarseness with a large pericardial effusion comes from impingement of which structure?","choices":["Thoracic duct","Recurrent laryngeal nerve","Hypoglossal nerve","Superior vena cava"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Large effusions are associated with dysphagia, nausea, hiccups, cough and hoarseness, the hoarseness from recurrent laryngeal nerve impingement. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which patient factor favors hospital admission for acute pericarditis?","choices":["Taking an oral anticoagulant","A recent upper respiratory infection","A history of controlled hypertension","Pain that began within the last day"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Patients on oral anticoagulants are higher risk, as are immunosuppressed patients and those with acute trauma, because bleeding into the pericardium can lead to tamponade. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Besides cardiology and cardiothoracic surgery, which service may an older adult need after a prolonged illness from pericardial disease?","choices":["Rehabilitation","Dermatology","Ophthalmology","Genetic counseling"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Older adults are more likely to need rehabilitation after prolonged illness, hospitalization and procedures; cardiology and cardiothoracic surgery cover imaging, pericardiocentesis and windows. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which jugular venous waveform pattern is described in cardiac tamponade?","choices":["Giant cannon a waves on every beat","Large v waves with each systole","Prominent x descent, absent y descent","Absent x descent, prominent y descent"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In tamponade the venous waveform shows a prominent x descent and an absent y descent, because early diastolic filling is blocked by the fluid under pressure. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"In the approach that uses C-reactive protein to guide therapy, when does tapering begin?","choices":["After a fixed 3 months, whatever the symptoms","Once symptom-free with a normal C-reactive protein","Once the friction rub can no longer be heard","After the electrocardiogram returns to normal"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In the C-reactive protein approach, the level is checked at presentation and then weekly, and tapering starts when the patient is symptom-free and the C-reactive protein has normalized. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What is the short-term outcome of cardiac tamponade that is recognized and treated promptly?","choices":["Decided only by age","Often good","Uniformly fatal","Poor, whatever the cause"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The short-term outcome of tamponade is often good with prompt recognition and treatment; the long-term prognosis depends on the underlying cause. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"Which procedure is performed for recurrent or posterior loculated pericardial effusions?","choices":["Pericardial window or pericardiectomy","Repeated needle pericardiocentesis","Thoracentesis of both pleural spaces","Coronary artery bypass grafting"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A pericardiectomy or pericardial window is performed for recurrent effusions and for loculated effusions, especially posterior ones that cannot be approached percutaneously. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Which treatment addresses uremic pericarditis?","choices":["Dialysis","Corticosteroids","Antituberculous drugs","Pericardiectomy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Treating the underlying disorder is the rule, and for uremic pericarditis that means dialysis, alongside the usual anti-inflammatory approach and close renal follow-up. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Uremic pericarditis is typically associated with a blood urea nitrogen above which level?","choices":["20 mg/dL","10 mg/dL","200 mg/dL","60 mg/dL"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Uremic pericarditis is linked to a blood urea nitrogen above 60 mg/dL; there will be laboratory evidence and a history of kidney disease, and it is treated with dialysis. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"How specific is pericardial calcification for constrictive pericarditis?","choices":["Highly specific but uncommon","Sensitive and nearly universal","Nonspecific and very common","Seen only after chest radiation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pericardial calcification is highly specific for constrictive pericarditis, especially with the right clinical picture, but it is uncommon: most patients have none on chest radiograph. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"How is a pericardial knock characterized diagnostically?","choices":["Common in most cases, but nonspecific","Heard in nearly every case of constriction","Heard only when tamponade is also present","Pathognomonic when present, but rare"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A pericardial knock is pathognomonic for constrictive pericarditis when present, but it is rare; most patients are recognized by an elevated jugular venous pressure and the Kussmaul sign. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"About what proportion of patients with acute pericarditis go on to recurrent pericarditis?","choices":["50 to 60%","Over 80%","15 to 30%","1 to 5%"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Recurrent pericarditis affects about 15 to 30% of patients with acute pericarditis, close to one in three, which is why adherence to the full treatment course matters. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which is a risk factor for multiple recurrences of pericarditis?","choices":["A low C-reactive protein","Mild gadolinium uptake","A prompt viral recovery","An autoimmune etiology"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Risk factors for multiple recurrences are the magnitude of C-reactive protein elevation, the severity of pericardial late gadolinium enhancement and an autoimmune etiology. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Acute pericarditis is inflammation of the pericardial sac that lasts less than how long?","choices":["3 months","1 year","4 to 6 weeks","2 to 3 days"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Acute pericarditis is inflammation of the pericardial sac lasting less than 4 to 6 weeks; symptoms beyond that without remission are called incessant, and beyond 3 months chronic. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which laboratory pair is drawn in the initial workup of acute pericarditis to gauge inflammation?","choices":["B-type natriuretic peptide and D-dimer","Lipase and hepatic transaminases","Blood cultures and procalcitonin","C-reactive protein and sedimentation rate"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The initial labs are a complete blood count, troponins, erythrocyte sedimentation rate and C-reactive protein; the C-reactive protein can also guide the length of therapy. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which feature separates a pleural rub from a pericardial friction rub?","choices":["It has a scratchy, squeaking quality","It grows louder on leaning forward","It stops when the breath is held","It is loudest at the left sternal border"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A pleural rub is tied to respiration, heard in the inspiratory phase, so it disappears with breath-holding; a pericardial rub persists and even intensifies while the breath is held. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Constriction that persists after a coexisting pericardial effusion has been drained defines which entity?","choices":["Recurrent pericarditis with effusion","Subacute cardiac tamponade","Loculated posterior pericardial effusion","Effusive-constrictive pericarditis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Effusive-constrictive pericarditis combines underlying constriction with a coexisting effusion, usually with tamponade, and the constriction persists after the effusion is drained. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which adverse effect of corticosteroids is a particular concern for older adults with pericarditis?","choices":["Hearing loss","Hypoglycemia","Osteoporosis","Bradycardia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Corticosteroids increase osteoporosis risk, a particular concern in older adults on prolonged courses; bone health has to be considered when steroids replace an anti-inflammatory. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"An elevated troponin in acute pericarditis suggests which process?","choices":["A purulent bacterial effusion","Developing constrictive physiology","A coexisting pulmonary embolism","Myocardial involvement (myopericarditis)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Troponin may be elevated if the myocardium is also affected, myopericarditis, and an elevated troponin is one of the features that favors hospitalization. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What role do intravenous fluids play in cardiac tamponade?","choices":["Contraindicated because of congestion","Only for tamponade after trauma","Temporizing: raise preload until drainage","Definitive therapy in most cases"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Intravenous fluid resuscitation increases preload and helps prevent right ventricular collapse; it is a temporizing, stabilizing measure until pericardiocentesis. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Which patients with constrictive pericarditis may first be given a trial of medical therapy?","choices":["Early, hemodynamically stable constriction","Long-standing, calcified constriction","Any constriction, whatever its stage","Any patient with severe anasarca"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Early constriction in a hemodynamically stable patient may get a 2-to-3-month trial of the acute pericarditis drugs for longer, with diuretics for fluid overload, before surgery. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Failure to respond to a nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug plus colchicine within 1 week suggests what?","choices":["Constriction that was missed at first","A cause other than idiopathic or viral","Normal recovery that only needs time","An allergy to the colchicine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Failure to respond within 1 week suggests a cause other than idiopathic or viral pericarditis, and no clinical improvement after 7 days of appropriately dosed therapy is a high-risk feature. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"What is the definitive treatment for cardiac tamponade?","choices":["Expedient pericardiocentesis","High-dose anti-inflammatories","Intravenous fluids alone","Diuresis with a loop diuretic"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Expedient pericardiocentesis is the definitive treatment for cardiac tamponade; it is both therapeutic and an opportunity to send the fluid for diagnostic studies. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Most pericardial disease is labeled idiopathic. What are most idiopathic cases thought to be?","choices":["Occult malignancy","Viral infections","Autoimmune disease","Bacterial infections"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Pericardial disease is most often idiopathic, and most idiopathic cases are likely viral; the specific virus is usually never isolated despite a preceding viral illness. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"How is the diagnosis of cardiac tamponade ultimately confirmed?","choices":["Pericardial thickening on tomography","Electrical alternans on a 12-lead tracing","Response to pericardial fluid drainage","A transthoracic echocardiogram alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Tamponade is a clinical diagnosis; the echocardiogram identifies the effusion and its hemodynamic significance, but the diagnosis is confirmed only by the hemodynamic and clinical response to drainage. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 57"}]};
